WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:14.789 align:start position:0%
 
please pray with me  spirit of the living god be here present  in our midst this morning today right  now this moment  for if you are here with us we know that  nothing else matters  but if you are not  then nothing else matters  in christ's name we pray amen  so when i think of easter i think of the  early morning sunlight and the empty  tomb  and the lilies and the whispers and the  spreading all and the ex hesitant but  excited joy of the disciples  i do not immediately think  of a glaring spotlight on a body that  splayed out in the middle of a dirt road  on the way to damascus with being  addressed by a voice booming from the  heavens  this is a different kind of easter  moment this morning  frederick buechner writes  of paul that his mads were madder and  his blues bluer  his pride prouder and his humbleness  humbler his strength stronger and his  weaknesses weaker than almost anybody  else's you'd be apt to think of  and the splash that he made when he fell  for christ is audible still  it's little wonder beekner says  that from the start he was a genius  at making enemies  even as a young man paul who was then  called saul in our text today he's  called saul  was already a standout leader  he was confident in his convictions  zealous and eager to prove himself he  took initiative and never shied away  from bold action  early on people began to look for to him  for approval and for direction  he's not unlike many yale students that  i know  it's as if he doused himself in charisma  daily as part of his morning ablutions  and a probably a healthy portion of it  too because rumor has it that paul  wasn't much to look at  he's described by others as being  bald-headed and bow-legged strongly  built but small in size with meeting  eyebrows and a rather large nose  and yet  his presence wafted before him and  behind him  everyone knew when he entered the room  he held the kind of power to sway a  crowd he was greatly admired i imagine  secretly envied and generally feared  our very first exposure to paul comes  in chapter 8 of the book of acts as he  is overseeing the stoning of stephen the  first of the christian disciples to die  for his confession  though you think that that may have been  enough to turn his stomach early on  he pressed on instead  ridding his world of chaos one christian  at a time  in fact he built his entire career on  keeping score he was driven by some  blend of ambition and self-righteousness  but perhaps also a little bit of  unacknowledged deep-seated sort of fear  with each arrest he was executing an  arbitrary retributive justice  that he needed to hold his identity  and his whole world together as a system  that made sense  but then it all unraveled on the road to  damascus  he heard the words come down from heaven  i am jesus whom you are persecuting  and i wonder how long the period at that  end of the sentence that breath in  between what came next  stretched out for paul as he lay there  i wonder if when he heard those words i  am jesus whom you are persecuting  if he saw immediately stephen's face  and maybe not just stephen  but all of the men and the women that he  had dragged from their homes and  synagogues across jerusalem and maybe  not just them either but all of the  people that he had exploited or abused  or even just pushed aside in his way to  the top  a huge crowd gathering around him  circling up  and i wonder if he wasn't holding his  breath  waiting  and wondering who would cast the first  stone  but instead of stones  more words came tumbling down from  heaven  it's a classic prophetic call did you  hear it get up and go  we know those words so well  and this is the easter moment because  the announcement of jesus's resurrection  for paul was also an accusation a  calling to account  but the words that follow it that  prophetic calling now get up and go  this is  a claiming a divine claiming and a  renewal of life for him  this is the easter message of both  divine justice and mercy embodied in the  one jesus christ  because in that moment paul's entire  notion of religion and justice  died  blind as he was i am sure that he could  see the ways that he had marred the  image of god and others and disfigured  it in himself as well  the systems that he had staked his life  on  suddenly seemed foolish  and small  and cruel before the cross and the  resurrection of jesus  now some christians have come to  describe their own experiences of  turning or repentance or conversion as a  sort of damascus road experience you've  heard this yes some of you might have  appropriated that language and feel  comfortable using it  so i hope that i can say without in any  way questioning the sincerity or the  legitimacy of those unique experiences  that i think that this particular easter  moment this damascus road story belongs  uniquely and solely to paul  what i mean is that we're not each of us  individual moses's called aside by a  burning bush we're not all paul's  who are  knocked down in the middle of the road  one day  i guess  his experience should neither be  like a rubric or a requirement in what  we count as christian discipleship  because just as paul himself has  emphasized in terms of the diversity of  the body the complex ways that it's so  complementary  we all encounter god's call in our lives  differently and distinctly for many of  us  much more subtly than this  we confess together regularly here in  the church because few of us have had  this kind of instantaneous turning that  paul has  we need the kind of structure and  support that we can offer each other  to name our failings and to turn again  and again repeatedly over time  in that sense i guess  meant for many of us  our liberation is often longer painfully  slower and more staggering than this  but as much as that moment was god's  unique call to paul  this easter message belongs to all of us  that christ is risen he is risen indeed  that we might be free  that we might be free from sin and  suffering and fear and death from  oppression and manipulation and  destruction and also free  from the ways that we have oppressed and  manipulated and aided in the destruction  of the image of god in others for all of  the suffering that we have called it  caused for the suffering that we have  also witnessed  either without the courage to speak or  the capacity to stop it  but once again also that we might be  free  from the million ways that we would  stone ourselves in judgment or despair  the one who created you the one who  redeemed you is the one who assures you  do not fear  i have redeemed you i have called you by  name  you are mine  christ is risen  he's risen indeed that we might be free  and christ calls us to a better way  paul walks away that day  days  days for days actually  but on the other side of it he emerges  as a fool for christ  baptized as a disciple he immediately  enters into the synagogues in damascus  the very ones that feared his coming  proclaiming jesus as the son of god  and his entire life plays out as this  sort of long ongoing confession  every time he confesses that jesus is  his savior he is confessing to right  along with it just what he has been  saved from  he boasts in his weaknesses as a living  example of god's forgiveness the power  of god's grace in jesus  and in this way paul goes right on being  his charismatic self  and making enemies left and right  only this time his charisma is founded  in a different kind of assurance and  ambition  it's no longer just this sort of  exercise of power  instead it's something closer to the  truth of the term  paul is charismatic because he has  himself become a vessel for the  outpouring of god's grace  he can't encounter another person  without seeing them as a beloved child  of god washed in grace and invited into  christ's bizarre sort of body  and knowing that god has chosen and  called and loved him even him he cannot  help but see them see them too  and he can't see them as such as  children of god beloved and grace washed  and filled and called he can't see them  that way without saying so to every  single person he encounters inviting and  exhorting them and saying look at me  look what god has done for me and know  that god can do this for you  there's no room for discretion in this  kind of mission  he's participating in god's salvation in  the world  of the world through christ one  christian at a time  may we all come to be so foolish  and so alive in christ this easter  amen

